# Dockerfile.dockerignore -- RAMEAU router build context
#
# Context root: services/router/ in this repository.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FILENAME IS LOAD-BEARING, AND IT WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-11-02
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker reads an ignore-file from the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT, or as
# `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile -- and nowhere else. This file lives
# in deploy/images/router/ while the context is services/router/, so for as long as it was
# named plain `.dockerignore` it was read by NOTHING, and every pattern below was inert:
# `COPY *.py ./` was bounded by nothing, and the "never, under any circumstances" block at
# the end of this file -- .env, *.pem, *.key, .netrc, .git-credentials -- was not in force at
# all.
#
# Measured rather than reasoned about. A `test_probe.py`, which the list below excludes, was
# planted in a stub context: it arrived in /app of the built image. Named to match the
# Dockerfile, as it now is, the same probe is excluded.
#
# So do not rename this file back, and do not move it without moving the Dockerfile with it.
# `git mv`-ing either one alone disarms this entire file, silently -- and the warning further
# down, that a green build is not evidence this list is right, applies to its NAME first.
#
# Deny-by-default, then re-admit. The point is not image size, it is that a narrow context is
# the only thing that makes the COPY groups in the Dockerfile mean anything: `COPY *.py ./`
# cannot widen what this file has already excluded.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO SYNTAX RULES THIS FILE OBEYS, BOTH LEARNED THE HARD WAY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. NO TRAILING COMMENTS ON A PATTERN LINE. A '#' starts a comment only at the START of a
#    line. Writing
#        !fleet_config.json              # read by config.py
#    is the single pattern "fleet_config.json<spaces>#<spaces>read by config.py", which
#    matches nothing, so the re-admission silently does not happen.
# 2. A NEGATION CANNOT RE-ADMIT A FILE WHOSE PARENT DIRECTORY IS EXCLUDED. `**` followed by
#    `!dir/file` does not work; the directory has to be re-admitted first.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A GREEN BUILD IS NOT EVIDENCE THIS LIST IS RIGHT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A module missing from this list does not fail the build. It fails at import or at first
# use, and /status can still answer 200. After every refresh of the router source, recompute
# the import closure of main:app with an AST walk and compare it against what this admits.
#
# And one module is NOT in that closure and must still be admitted: bifrost_delegate.py is
# read off disk with `ast` and imported by nothing. Omit it and the tool loop refuses every
# delegated leaf, fail-closed, while health still reads green. It is admitted explicitly
# below, with this comment, because it is the one entry a closure-based regeneration of this
# file would drop.

# ---- deny everything, then re-admit ----
**

# ---- dependency manifest ----
!requirements.txt

# ---- application source ----
# Bounded to the top level on purpose: the router's modules are flat, and `**/*.py` would
# re-admit test files, scratch scripts and vendored copies.
!*.py

# ---- packages ----
!backends/
!backends/**

# ---- the skill registry, baked at build time ----
!skills/
!skills/**

# ---- NOT admitted, and deliberately so ----
# The four runtime JSON files are absent from this context by design. The release ships them
# as *.example.json and the stack bind-mounts the operator's copies; see the Dockerfile and
# deploy/compose.yaml. Do not re-admit them: baking fleet detail into a published image is
# the thing the release exists to avoid.
#
# NAMED AS PATTERNS, not left to `**`, and recursive -- added 2026-11-03. This section was
# prose only, which meant the four files were kept out at the CONTEXT ROOT and nowhere else:
# `backends/**` and `skills/**` above re-admit wholesale, and `**` has already been
# overridden by the time a file inside them is considered. Measured on this file AS IT
# STOOD BEFORE 04a4eb1 -- it is byte-identical from 1d69a59 through 5bb4983, so any
# of those five commits shows the same bytes: `backends/fleet_config.json`,
# `backends/capability_profiles.json`, `backends/bifrost_shell_policy.json` and
# `skills/capability_registry.json` all arrived in the built image while the root
# `fleet_config.json` was correctly excluded. A reader testing the current file will and
# should see the opposite.
#
# The four are named ONE BY ONE rather than as a blanket `**/*.json`, for two reasons and
# only the second applies to this list. First, on the PORTAL's list a blanket would sit
# below `!package.json` and `!package-lock.json` and deny the portal its own manifest --
# this list re-admits no JSON at all, so that hazard is the other file's, and an earlier
# draft of this comment wrongly generalised it to both by citing the `!requirements.txt`
# re-admission, which is not JSON. Second, and the reason here: naming them is the only
# form that says WHICH files this is about, and the four are the subject of note 1 in the
# Dockerfile and of all four deploy/config/*.example.json.
**/fleet_config.json
**/capability_registry.json
**/capability_profiles.json
**/bifrost_shell_policy.json

# ---- never, under any circumstances ----
# THIS BLOCK'S GUARANTEE RESTS ON THREE PROPERTIES, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS GATED.
#   REACH     -- the patterns are recursive, so they apply inside `backends/` and `skills/`,
#                which this file re-admits wholesale.   Fixed 1d69a59. NOT gated: the
#                probes that measured it were throwaway stub contexts built outside git
#                and discarded, and no step builds a context or tests admission.
#   FINALITY  -- nothing re-admits below this block, because a .dockerignore is
#                last-match-wins.                       Fixed and GATED in 04a4eb1.
#   SHAPE COVERAGE -- the shapes listed below are the ones worth denying. NOT gated, and
#                resting on prose. The heading says "never, under any circumstances" and
#                what follows is about a dozen shapes, which is a weaker thing than the
#                heading claims. This repository's own .gitignore names shapes this list
#                does not, and `docker build` reads the WORKING TREE rather than the index,
#                so a gitignored credential is invisible to every git-based gate here and
#                still enters the image. Recorded in RELEASE-SCOPE.md as the top residual
#                risk rather than patched in passing, because closing it is a decision
#                about what this list is FOR.
#
# ONE OF THREE, NOT TWO. This heading said "ONLY TWO OF THEM ARE GATED" until 2026-11-03,
# and that was false when written: only FINALITY has a step behind it. REACH rests on
# probes that no longer exist anywhere, which is the same "promise rather than property"
# this block was added to be candid about -- found inside the note about that defect.
#
# Every pattern here is RECURSIVE (`**/`), and that is the whole point of the block rather
# than a style choice. The stated purpose is that a re-admission above cannot sweep one of
# these in as a side effect -- and `backends/**` and `skills/**` above are exactly such
# re-admissions, wholesale.
#
# A ROOT-ANCHORED pattern cannot reach into them. Docker matches an ignore pattern against
# the whole context-relative path and a single `*` does not cross a `/`, so `*.pem` matches
# `foo.pem` and NOT `backends/foo.pem`. Until 2026-11-03 every line below except the two
# __pycache__/pyc ones was root-anchored, so the block was in force only at the top level --
# which is the one place `**` already covered it. Where it was actually needed it did nothing.
#
# MEASURED, not reasoned about, in a throwaway stub context built outside git and discarded:
# with the previous root-anchored set, `backends/inner.pem`, `backends/inner.key` and
# `skills/inner.pem` all arrived in the built image, while `root.pem` and `root.key` were
# correctly excluded. With the recursive set below the same five are excluded and
# `backends/mod.py`, `main.py` and `requirements.txt` still arrive. `**/X` was confirmed to
# subsume `X`, so the root-anchored twins are not kept alongside: they would be redundant,
# and two spellings of one rule invite an edit to half of it.
#
# The two `**/__pycache__/` and `**/*.pyc` lines were already in this form, which is the
# evidence that the rule was known and simply not applied to the credential shapes.
#
# The test patterns are recursive for the same reason. RELEASE-SCOPE.md's import closure
# records the shipping set as 8 top-level `.py` plus 4 under `backends/`, with none of the
# 12 matching `test_*.py` or `*_test.py`, so this excludes nothing the release ships.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/id_rsa
**/id_ed25519
**/.netrc
**/.git-credentials
**/.git/
**/.github/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.log
**/*.bak
**/*.bak-*
**/*.sqlite
**/*.db
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/node_modules/
**/tests/
**/test_*.py
**/*_test.py

# ---- WIDENED 2026-11-03 to the same set as the portal's ----
# The portal's file carries the full reasoning for this block and is the one John ruled on
# (in chat, 2026-11-03: *"2. widen to the portal"*). This list follows it in the same
# sitting rather than later, for the reason the portal's own note gives: the two credential
# sets are MEANT to be one set, they diverged once for no recorded reason, and a widening
# applied to one of two files re-creates exactly that divergence. The gate added with this
# commit now refuses it: it requires EVERY never-block to carry every shape in
# `deploy/images/NEVER-BLOCK-SHAPES.txt`, so neither file can drift below that set alone.
#
# It does NOT require the two blocks to be identical, and saying so would be false. They
# differ outside the credential classes and should: this one denies `**/__pycache__/`,
# `**/.venv/` and the test shapes, the portal's denies `**/*_fixed.jsx` and `**/*_v2.jsx`.
# What each file adds beyond the declared set is its own image's business. The declared set
# is the floor, not the definition.
#
# Twenty-four patterns, every one a line of `.gitignore`'s "environment and secrets",
# "keys and certificates" and "tokens" sections that had no `**/` twin here. Derived by
# comparing the files line by line, not by thinking of more shapes. None of them matches
# any tracked file, and RELEASE-SCOPE.md's import closure -- 8 top-level `.py` plus 4
# under `backends/` -- contains nothing of these shapes either, so nothing the release
# ships is denied.
#
# `.gitignore`'s assembly class (`_scrub/`, `_preimages/`, `_defect-fixes/`,
# `WIPE-LEDGER.md`) is deliberately NOT here: compose builds this image with
# `context: ../services/router`, so every pattern in this file is relative to
# `services/router/` and those four live at the repository root, outside it. They would be
# four inert lines reading as four guarantees. The model-weights class is absent as a
# scope rather than a credential matter; RELEASE-SCOPE.md owns it.
#
# `**/.npmrc` has no job on a python image and is kept anyway, because the gate compares
# the two lists for equality and one list with a hole in it is how they diverged before.
# `**/.pypirc` is the one that earns its place here: it carries index credentials for pip.
**/*.env
**/secrets/
**/.secrets/
**/*.secret
**/*.secrets
**/credentials.json
**/*credential*
**/*-credentials*
**/auth.json
**/.sops.yaml
**/.sops/
**/*.kubeconfig
**/kubeconfig
**/kubeconfig.*
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa.*
**/id_ed25519.*
**/*.ppk
**/known_hosts
**/*.token
**/_netrc
**/.npmrc
**/.pypirc
